{
  "dom_id": "step-q343322",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14211452/assets/e700536730a443e47e56bf33cd7dcb460032b72984d5aaaaf75d1de987c6b495.png)\n\nA factory uses a rectangular mold with a fixed width of $18\\,\\text{in}$ and a fixed height of $10\\,\\text{in}.$ Liquid resin is poured in so that the resin fills the mold and the length increases at a rate of $3\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}$ as the front face moves. What is the rate of change of the resin's volume in the mold?\n\nThe resin's volume is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$",
  "worked_solution": "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $H$ and $W$ be the height and width of the rectangular mold, respectively, and\n\n- let $L$ and $V$ be the length and volume of the resin, respectively.\n\nNote that $L$ and $V$ change with time, while $H$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nH=10\\,\\text{in},\\,W=18\\,\\text{in},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=3\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $L,$ is given by the formula for the volume of resin:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ treating both $H$ and $W$ as constants, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =WH\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(18)(10)\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =180\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =540.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resin's volume is increasing at a rate of $540\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
